course: dictionary
lesson: 10488
title: Turmeric and context clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Turmeric is a noun for a spice, a powder that adds flavour and colour to food. You can use context clues to work out what it means. «Yellow turmeric turns reddish-brown with an alkali, so a curry stain can turn red when washed with soap.» The words yellow, curry and stain tell you that turmeric is a yellow thing that can mark food.
explain: The clue «turns reddish-brown with an alkali» tells you that turmeric changes colour when it meets an alkali. Soap is alkaline, so the sentence says a curry stain can turn red when you wash it with soap. You do not need to know every science word to see the pattern.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: "Yellow turmeric turns reddish-brown..." Does it still make sense? Yes, so your guess is probably right. Then you can look it up to be sure.
fact: Turmeric is a bright yellow-orange spice made from the dried and ground root of a plant in the ginger family.
fact: Turmeric is used in curries and to colour food, which is why many curries look yellow.
fact: India grows most of the world's turmeric.
fact: Turmeric stains fingers and clothes yellow, and it turns red-brown in an alkali, so scientists use it as an indicator in experiments.
fact: Some people sell turmeric as a cure for many illnesses, but scientists have not shown strong proof that it cures diseases.
exercise: What is turmeric? | A loud musical instrument from India | *A bright yellow-orange spice made from a plant root | A soap used for washing clothes | A red fruit that grows on trees
exercise: «A curry stain can turn red when washed with soap.» Why does the stain change colour? | Curry is cooked in cold water | Soap is sweet and sticky | *Soap is an alkali, and turmeric changes colour in an alkali | Turmeric is always red
exercise: Which country grows most of the world's turmeric? | Scotland | *India | Brazil | Australia
test: Turmeric can be used as an indicator in experiments. What does indicator mean here? | A person who writes down the results | A loud noise made during an experiment | *A substance whose colour changes to show what something is | A test that never changes colour
test: Some people say turmeric cures many illnesses. What do scientists say? | Scientists have banned all spices | Scientists have shown that it cures diseases | Scientists say it has no colour | *Scientists have not shown strong proof that it cures diseases
exam: Read «Yellow turmeric turns reddish-brown with an alkali.» What does this tell you about turmeric? | *It changes colour when it meets an alkali | It only grows in one town | It is a type of cloth | It is always green
